    <section id="purpose">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">01 — Foundations</span>
        <h2>Purpose &amp; Origin</h2>
        <p>What the standard is, why it exists, and what "certifiable" actually means here.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>What it is</h3>
          <p>ISO/IEC 42001:2023 is the first international standard specifying requirements for establishing, implementing, maintaining, and continually improving an <strong>AI Management System (AIMS)</strong> within an organization. It was published in December 2023 by ISO and IEC's joint technical committee (JTC 1/SC 42), the same body that works on AI standards broadly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Why it was created</h3>
          <p>Organizations building or using AI needed a management-system standard that plugs into what they already run — quality management (ISO 9001), information security (ISO/IEC 27001) — rather than a bespoke AI-only process. 42001 gives auditors, regulators, and business partners a common, checkable structure for "does this organization actually manage its AI risk," not just "does its AI perform well."</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Who it's for</h3>
          <p>Any organization that provides or uses products or services utilizing AI systems, regardless of size or sector — from a startup shipping one model to a multinational with dozens of AI-enabled products across its supply chain.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>What "certifiable" means</h3>
          <p>Unlike the NIST AI RMF, which is voluntary guidance, an organization can hire an accredited third-party certification body to audit its AIMS against 42001 and issue a certificate — similar to how organizations get ISO 27001-certified for information security today.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="how-to-use">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">02 — Using this guide</span>
        <h2>How to Use This Guide</h2>
        <p>This tool summarizes a management-system standard for a course session — it is not a substitute for an implementation project or a certification audit.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>Start with the PDCA wheel.</strong> Everything below sits somewhere on Plan–Do–Check–Act. If you remember which quadrant a clause lives in, you'll remember what it's for.</li>
        <li><strong>Read Clauses 4–10 as one continuous document.</strong> Unlike NIST AI RMF's iterative functions, these clauses are a genuine numbered sequence — each builds on commitments made in the one before it (you can't do Clause 8 Operation without the scope Clause 4 defined).</li>
        <li><strong>Treat Annex A as "what," not "how."</strong> Annex A controls are reference points an auditor checks for evidence against — they are deliberately terse. The organization decides how to actually implement each one.</li>
        <li><strong>Compare it against NIST AI RMF.</strong> Use the comparison table below — many programs run both: NIST AI RMF for the risk-thinking, ISO/IEC 42001 for the certifiable management structure around it.</li>
        <li><strong>Cite the primary standard for graded work.</strong> ISO standards are copyrighted and must be purchased from ISO or a national standards body — this guide paraphrases publicly available structure and commentary, not licensed clause text.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

