    <section class="clause-list" id="clause4">
      <div class="clause-band">
        <span class="phase-tag phase-plan">Plan</span>
        <h2><span class="clause-code">Clause</span> 4 — Context of the Organization</h2>
        <p class="intent">Before anything else, the organization has to define what the AIMS actually covers — its boundaries, its stakeholders, and the issues that could affect it.</p>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">4.1</span><span class="sub-title">Understanding the organization and its context</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Internal and external issues relevant to the organization's purpose that affect its ability to achieve the AIMS's intended outcomes — market pressures, technical capability, regulatory environment.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">4.2</span><span class="sub-title">Needs and expectations of interested parties</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Identifying who has a stake in the organization's AI — customers, regulators, employees, affected communities — and what each expects.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">4.3</span><span class="sub-title">Determining the scope of the AIMS</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>A documented, bounded statement of which AI systems, business units, and activities the management system actually covers — the single most consequential decision in an implementation, since everything else is scoped to it.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">4.4</span><span class="sub-title">AI management system established</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>The organization establishes, implements, maintains, and continually improves the AIMS as an actual functioning system — not a document that sits on a shelf.</p></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CLAUSE 5 -->
    <section class="clause-list" id="clause5">
      <div class="clause-band">
        <span class="phase-tag phase-plan">Plan</span>
        <h2><span class="clause-code">Clause</span> 5 — Leadership</h2>
        <p class="intent">Top management has to own the AIMS visibly — a management system with no leadership commitment is the most common reason implementations fail an audit.</p>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">5.1</span><span class="sub-title">Leadership and commitment</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Top management demonstrates leadership by integrating AIMS requirements into business processes, ensuring resources are available, and communicating the importance of effective AI management.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">5.2</span><span class="sub-title">AI policy</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>A documented, top-management-approved policy stating the organization's commitments around responsible AI — communicated internally and available to interested parties.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">5.3</span><span class="sub-title">Roles, responsibilities and authorities</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Specific roles for the AIMS are assigned and communicated — someone is accountable for conformity, someone reports on performance to top management.</p></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CLAUSE 6 -->
    <section class="clause-list" id="clause6">
      <div class="clause-band">
        <span class="phase-tag phase-plan">Plan</span>
        <h2><span class="clause-code">Clause</span> 6 — Planning</h2>
        <p class="intent">Where AI-specific risk assessment and impact assessment enter the standard — this is the clause that most distinguishes 42001 from a generic management-system standard.</p>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">6.1</span><span class="sub-title">Actions to address risks and opportunities</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Includes a formal AI risk assessment process and an AI system impact assessment process — evaluating potential consequences for individuals and society, not only the organization.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">6.2</span><span class="sub-title">AI objectives and planning to achieve them</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Measurable objectives, consistent with the AI policy, with a plan for who does what, by when, with what resources.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">6.3</span><span class="sub-title">Planning of changes</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Changes to the AIMS are made in a planned manner — an organization doesn't get to quietly redefine its scope or process mid-cycle.</p></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CLAUSE 7 -->
    <section class="clause-list" id="clause7">
      <div class="clause-band">
        <span class="phase-tag phase-do">Do</span>
        <h2><span class="clause-code">Clause</span> 7 — Support</h2>
        <p class="intent">The resourcing clause — people, competence, awareness, communication, and documentation that make the plan executable.</p>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">7.1–7.2</span><span class="sub-title">Resources and competence</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>The organization determines and provides the resources needed for the AIMS, and ensures people doing AI-related work under its control are competent — through education, training, or experience.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">7.3–7.4</span><span class="sub-title">Awareness and communication</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Relevant people are aware of the AI policy and their contribution to the AIMS's effectiveness; internal and external communications about the AIMS are planned, not ad hoc.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">7.5</span><span class="sub-title">Documented information</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Required records — policies, risk assessments, impact assessments, audit results — are created, controlled, and kept available; this is what an auditor actually reviews.</p></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CLAUSE 8 -->
    <section class="clause-list" id="clause8">
      <div class="clause-band">
        <span class="phase-tag phase-do">Do</span>
        <h2><span class="clause-code">Clause</span> 8 — Operation</h2>
        <p class="intent">Where the plans from Clause 6 actually get carried out, across the AI system lifecycle.</p>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">8.1</span><span class="sub-title">Operational planning and control</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Processes needed to meet AIMS requirements are planned, implemented, and controlled — including managing planned changes and reviewing unintended ones.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">8.2</span><span class="sub-title">AI risk assessment</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Performed at planned intervals or upon significant change — not just once before launch.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">8.3</span><span class="sub-title">AI risk treatment</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Implementing the risk treatment plan developed in Clause 6 — the point where risk decisions become real action.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">8.4</span><span class="sub-title">AI system impact assessment</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Carried out for individual AI systems as they're developed or before significant changes — evaluating impact on individuals, groups, and society.</p></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CLAUSE 9 -->
    <section class="clause-list" id="clause9">
      <div class="clause-band">
        <span class="phase-tag phase-check">Check</span>
        <h2><span class="clause-code">Clause</span> 9 — Performance Evaluation</h2>
        <p class="intent">The clause an external auditor will spend the most time on — proof that the organization actually checks its own work.</p>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">9.1</span><span class="sub-title">Monitoring, measurement, analysis, evaluation</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>The organization determines what needs monitoring, the methods used, and when results are analyzed — evidence-based, not anecdotal.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">9.2</span><span class="sub-title">Internal audit</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Planned internal audits, conducted by people independent of what they're auditing, to check the AIMS conforms to the organization's own requirements and the standard's.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">9.3</span><span class="sub-title">Management review</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>Top management reviews the AIMS at planned intervals to ensure it remains suitable, adequate, and effective — closing the loop back to Clause 5 Leadership.</p></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CLAUSE 10 -->
    <section class="clause-list" id="clause10">
      <div class="clause-band">
        <span class="phase-tag phase-act">Act</span>
        <h2><span class="clause-code">Clause</span> 10 — Improvement</h2>
        <p class="intent">What happens when Clause 9 finds something wrong — and how the whole cycle feeds back into Clause 4.</p>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">10.1</span><span class="sub-title">Continual improvement</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>The organization continually improves the AIMS's suitability, adequacy, and effectiveness — the standard expects the system to keep getting better, not just stay compliant.</p></div></div></div>

        <div class="accordion" data-open="false"><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false"><span class="label"><span class="sub-code">10.2</span><span class="sub-title">Nonconformity and corrective action</span></span><span class="chevron">▾</span></button><div class="acc-panel"><div class="acc-panel-inner"><p>When something doesn't conform, the organization reacts to it, evaluates the need for action to eliminate the cause, and reviews whether the corrective action actually worked.</p></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="annex">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">04 — Reference controls</span>
        <h2>Annex A — Controls</h2>
        <p>Annex A works like ISO/IEC 27001's Annex A: a reference set of controls an organization selects from, based on its own risk assessment, and documents in a Statement of Applicability. These are terse by design — Annex A says <em>what</em>, not <em>how</em>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="annex-grid">
        <div class="annex-card"><span class="a-code">A.2</span><h4>Policies related to AI</h4><p>AI policy content, review, and alignment with other organizational policies.</p></div>
        <div class="annex-card"><span class="a-code">A.3</span><h4>Internal organization</h4><p>Roles and responsibilities specific to AI, reporting lines, segregation of duties.</p></div>
        <div class="annex-card"><span class="a-code">A.4</span><h4>Resources for AI systems</h4><p>Data, tooling, system, and human resources allocated to AI systems and their documentation.</p></div>
        <div class="annex-card"><span class="a-code">A.5</span><h4>Assessing impacts of AI systems</h4><p>Impact assessment process, considering effects on individuals, groups, and society, including environmental impact.</p></div>
        <div class="annex-card"><span class="a-code">A.6</span><h4>AI system life cycle</h4><p>Controls spanning design, development, verification and validation, deployment, operation, monitoring, and decommissioning.</p></div>
        <div class="annex-card"><span class="a-code">A.7</span><h4>Data for AI systems</h4><p>Data quality, provenance, and acquisition practices feeding AI systems.</p></div>
        <div class="annex-card"><span class="a-code">A.8</span><h4>Information for interested parties</h4><p>Transparency obligations — what's communicated to users, regulators, and the public about a given AI system.</p></div>
        <div class="annex-card"><span class="a-code">A.9</span><h4>Use of AI systems</h4><p>Responsible use practices, including use of third-party AI systems within the organization.</p></div>
        <div class="annex-card"><span class="a-code">A.10</span><h4>Third-party and customer relationships</h4><p>Controls covering suppliers, partners, and customer-facing commitments around AI.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="classroom">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">05 — In the classroom</span>
        <h2>Classroom Uses</h2>
        <p>Ways instructors have used this structure with students.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Gap-analysis exercise</h3>
          <p>Give students a fictional (or their own institution's) AI use case and have them identify, clause by clause, what documentation would already exist versus what would need to be created from scratch.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Statement of Applicability drill</h3>
          <p>Assign a handful of Annex A controls and have students argue whether each is "applicable" to a given scenario and why — mirroring the real Statement of Applicability exercise auditors expect.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Mock internal audit</h3>
          <p>Pair students up: one drafts evidence for a clause (e.g., Clause 9 monitoring records), the other plays internal auditor and probes for gaps.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Cross-framework mapping</h3>
          <p>Have students map one 42001 clause or Annex A control to the closest NIST AI RMF category, then discuss where the mapping is clean and where it isn't.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="sources">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">06 — Go to the source</span>
        <h2>Sources &amp; Further Reading</h2>
        <p>This guide is a teaching summary. ISO standards are copyrighted texts — cite and quote from the official standard for anything graded.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>ISO/IEC 42001:2023</h3>
          <p>The official standard, purchasable through ISO or a national standards body.<br><code>iso.org — search "ISO/IEC 42001"</code></p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>ISO/IEC JTC 1/SC 42</h3>
          <p>The joint technical committee developing 42001 and related AI standards — useful for context on where 42001 sits among other AI standards work.<br><code>iso.org/committee/6794475.html</code></p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Accredited certification bodies</h3>
          <p>For understanding what an actual certification audit involves, check national accreditation body listings for organizations authorized to certify against 42001.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>
</div>

